# COMPOSITE-TOKEN-HIJACK (docs/DESIGN.md Appendix A; docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 6;
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.2).  Deferred at §13 step 1 as findings F5/F20: seeding
# it before its contributors existed was a guaranteed E051 lint failure.  All
# three now ship - CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_LONG_EXPIRY-01 (§8.5, CLOUD-23),
# DAST-LEAK-JS_CONFIG-01 (§7.1, DAST-10) and DAST-GQL-INTROSPECTION-01 (§7.4,
# DAST-27) - so the seed lands here rather than staying a fixture-only proof.
#
# The chain is a property of one deployed front end: a long-lived AppSync API
# key (a static, unrotated bearer credential), that same class of key served in
# the JavaScript bundle a browser downloaded (so the credential is not merely
# long-lived but already exposed to anyone who fetched the page), and GraphQL
# introspection left enabled (so whoever now holds the key also holds a
# complete map of every type, field and mutation it can reach). Each
# contributor alone is a medium; together they are the critical finding a
# reader acts on first. All three are REQUIRED - this is one chain, not a set
# of interchangeable alternatives - so every contributor is `requires`, and
# `correlate-on: target` is the only correlation key all three can supply
# (DAST via `loc_target`; the cloud contributor via §9.2.2's endpoint-host
# attribution against config/scope.conf).

id: COMPOSITE-TOKEN-HIJACK
kind: derived
title: A long-lived AppSync API key is exposed in served JavaScript, and its schema is fully queryable via introspection
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-522
owasp: A07:2021
requires: CLOUD-APPSYNC-API_KEY_LONG_EXPIRY-01
requires: DAST-LEAK-JS_CONFIG-01
requires: DAST-GQL-INTROSPECTION-01
correlate-on: target
tags: derived
remediation: Rotate the AppSync API key immediately - it is not merely
  long-lived, it has already been observed served to every visitor of the
  bundle, so treat it as compromised rather than merely stale. Then close all
  three links so the chain cannot re-form: move the API off API_KEY
  authentication onto AWS_IAM or Amazon Cognito user pools so a captured
  credential alone is no longer sufficient; stop shipping the key to the
  browser and have it held server-side instead; and disable introspection on
  the production API so a future leaked key does not also hand out a complete
  map of what it can reach. Any one of these three closes the composite, but
  only rotating the key addresses the credential already exposed.
references: CWE-522
references: CWE-613
references: CWE-540
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A07_2021-Identification_and_Authentication_Failures/

# COMPOSITE-IMAGE-EFFECTIVE_ROOT and the three COMPOSITE-IMAGE-STALE_BASE_*
# records below it (IMG-14; rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.2/§9.2.2).
# `modules/iac/dockerfile.rules` reads
# what a Dockerfile's AUTHOR wrote; `modules/image/` reads what the BUILT
# ARTIFACT actually ships. Neither can see what the other sees, so correlating the two says something stronger than
# either alone: the source's omission was really left uncorrected in the
# artifact, or the artifact's unpatched packages really do trace back to an
# unpinned base a rebuild would have refreshed. `correlate-on: file` joins
# them on the Dockerfile's scan-root-relative path - the `IAC-DOCKER-*` side
# supplies it automatically (its `path` fingerprint profile always sets
# `corr_file` from its own `loc_path`, lib/findings.sh's
# `_finding_fill_correlation`); the `IMAGE-*` side supplies it only when the
# operator declares `config/images.conf`'s optional `dockerfile` key
# (§9.6.8) naming that same path, per §9.2.2's new IMAGE row. Per §9.2's own
# "correlate-on: none/target/etc. are the only join keys, and a contributor
# that cannot produce one simply has no value" rule, an image scanned with
# no declared `dockerfile` never joins - never a guessed pairing.
#
# COMPOSITE-IMAGE-EFFECTIVE_ROOT is one chain, not a set of alternatives, so
# both contributors are `requires`: IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01 (the Dockerfile
# has no non-root USER instruction) confirmed by IMAGE-CFG-RUNS_AS_ROOT-01
# (the built image's own merged config confirms the EFFECTIVE runtime user
# really is root, across every base layer and multi-stage copy a source
# lint cannot see through). Each alone is a real finding already registered
# under its own id; together they close the gap between them -
# "IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01 reads one Dockerfile's own USER instruction; the
# effective user comes from the merged config across every base layer" - so
# the composite is the CONFIRMED case, not merely the suspected one.
#
# COMPOSITE-IMAGE-STALE_BASE_APK/_DPKG/_RPM say the analogous thing for
# package freshness: "the unpinned base is delivering stale vulnerable
# packages." Splitting it by distro/package-manager rather than seeding one
# record is what the primitives here actually let this say correctly.
# `requires`/`any-of` on ONE record express (ALL requires) AND (>=1 of
# any-of); there is no way to express (>=1 of GROUP A) AND (>=1 of GROUP B)
# on one record, and the two groups here are genuinely both disjunctions -
# either IAC-DOCKER-* check can be the unpinned-base signal (a Dockerfile
# pinned to `:latest@sha256:...` trips LATEST_TAG but not UNPINNED_DIGEST,
# so neither implies the other), and any distro's own IMAGE-PKG-* check can
# be the vulnerable-package signal, but a single image is Alpine XOR
# Debian/Ubuntu XOR RHEL/Fedora (never more than one distro's package
# database at once), so the three IMAGE-PKG-* ids are mutually exclusive IN
# PRACTICE within one image's own findings. Fixing the package side to a
# single `requires` entry per record and leaving the base-pinning side as
# `any-of` expresses the correct AND-of-two-ORs without any engine change.
# IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-03 (rpm) is a real, registered check id
# today (E051 asks only that a contributor id exist, never that it already
# fires) even though `modules/image/checks-rpm.rules`' own header states
# nothing calls it yet - seeding its composite now costs nothing and is
# already correct the day a future rpm advisory-matcher ticket wires it in,
# the identical "registered, not yet reachable" precedent that file's own
# header already documents for the check itself.

id: COMPOSITE-IMAGE-EFFECTIVE_ROOT
kind: derived
title: A Dockerfile's missing non-root USER is confirmed by the built image's own effective runtime user
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-250
owasp: A04:2021
requires: IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01
requires: IMAGE-CFG-RUNS_AS_ROOT-01
correlate-on: file
tags: derived
remediation: Add (or fix) a non-root USER instruction in the Dockerfile that
  builds this image's final stage - or in whichever base image is meant to
  set one - then rebuild and re-scan both the source and the artifact. This
  is not merely a lint finding: the built image's own config confirms the
  container actually runs as root today, so treat it as a live privilege
  exposure rather than a stylistic gap. A base-image bump can change the
  effective user with no Dockerfile line changing at all, so re-check this
  after any FROM tag/digest bump too.
references: CWE-250
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A04_2021-Insecure_Design/

id: COMPOSITE-IMAGE-STALE_BASE_APK
kind: derived
title: An unpinned base image is shipping known-vulnerable apk packages
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
requires: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-01
any-of: IAC-DOCKER-LATEST_TAG-01
any-of: IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01
correlate-on: file
tags: derived
remediation: The Dockerfile's own base image reference is not pinned to an
  immutable tag and content digest, and the built image already carries a
  package with a known, published advisory - the two are connected: an
  unpinned base is exactly what lets a routine rebuild silently reintroduce
  a vulnerable version instead of refreshing it away. Pin the base image
  with an explicit release tag AND an `@sha256:...` digest
  (IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01's own remediation), then rebuild against a
  current base and re-scan to confirm the advisory clears.
references: CWE-1104
references: CWE-829
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/

id: COMPOSITE-IMAGE-STALE_BASE_DPKG
